==Evolution==
Evolves into '''Dancing Flasher Cat''' at level 10.

Evolves into '''Can Can Cat''' at level 30 using [[Catfruit]] and XP.

==Strategy/Usage==
At first glance, Hip Hop Cat may seem like a mediocre crowd-controller due to its low chance to slow Red enemies and slow Single Target attacks. However, Hip Hop Cat's main use lies in delivering DPS comparable to the likes of [[Bahamut Cat (Special Cat)|Bahamut Cat]] or other high-cost units due to high attack power, health and short recharge time for a meager 675¢. Against short-ranged enemies such as [[Nimoy Bore]], they can easily stack up and take enemies down very quickly. Treat the slow as a nice bonus that may help against [[Bore]], [[Shy Boy]] or [[One Horn]], but don't rely on it; if stalling Reds is your goal, it would generally be more effective to use [[Witch Cat (Rare Cat)|Witch Cat]] or [[Apple Cat (Super Rare Cat)|iCat]]. Due to being a stat-based unit, Hip Hop Cat is often worth your [[Catseye]] investment, on par with other important units like [[Crazed Tank Cat (Super Rare Cat)|Crazed Tank Cat]].

Think of Hip Hop Cat as a more cost-efficient version of [[Lesser Demon Cat (Uber Rare Cat)|Lesser Demon Cat]]—they have very high health and damage for their price, but are hampered by short range and Single Attack. It's great as a disposable hard-hitter throughout the early stages of the game, and shines even more later on. Get in one good hit, and you'll cripple or outright kill just any workable enemy if there's the chance. Hip Hop Cat is extremely helpful in dealing with enemies with slow attack rates, such as [[THE SLOTH]], [[Sunfish Jones]] and [[Le'noir]], provided that there are no peons or dangerous support in the way.

==='''True Form'''===
Though not as flashy as other True Forms in its changes, Hip Hop Cat now becomes a staple melee unit. Can Can Cat's health and damage stats are now boosted by 50%, making them an even stronger generalist. This True Form also increases its slow chance by 20%, though it still remains a small nice bonus taken for granted rather than for typical crowd-control. Can Can Cat also gains a situational ability, [[Immune to Warp]]. At the very least, this helps with things like dismissing [[Everlord Wanwan]]'s super long Warp gimmick.

==='''Talents'''===
Through Talents, Can Can Cat can unlock the Money Up ability. This ability is considered one of the most important Talents in the game, as Can Can's slow yet powerful Single Attacks allows it to easier stall peons for and pick them off one by one for more income. The cash boost is particularly effective on peons with high money drops, such as [[Doge Dark]] and [[Celeboodle]]. Defense Buff and Attack Buff are undeniably useful, but it's more efficient to boost with Catseyes and (+) levels first. The Move Speed Up Talent also allows Can Can Cat to more easily "dance" into the blind spots of [[:Category:Long Distance Enemies|Long Distance enemies]], tackle further-ranged enemies, especially those with very long attack animations, or simply to reach the front lines quicker, allowing it to get more, and/or earlier hits and putting its great stats to good use. Lastly, Target [[:Category:Alien Enemies|Alien]] is not usually practical whatsoever. Due to Can Can's long time between attacks, as well as being single target and only having a 60% chance to proc the effect, there will almost always be a severe lack of uptime. The likes of [[Psychocat (Rare Cat)|Corrupted Psychocat]] and [[Surfer Cat (Super Rare Cat)|Seafarer Cat]] fill the role of Alien debuffer significantly better, and your NP should be spent elsewhere. Consequently this talent's most practical use case is to allow Can Can to substitute for another cat when a slot cannot be spared for another crowd controller like in [[Hunt for the Xenobeast]].

==='''Orb'''===
It is not recommended that you give Can Can an Effect [[Talent Orbs|Talent Orb]]. However, to push the usage of its Money Up Talent, you can equip a Dark Attack Up Orb or Relic Attack Up Orb for higher magnification Doge Darks or [[Sir Rel]]s respectively. A Red Attack Up Orb would pair well with their slow and match-up against Reds, which tend to be tanky and low-range.

For ability orbs, Single Surge Counter is quite useful, but is reliant on the presence of Surges. Shortened Cooldown Orb can be used in tandem with research Cat Combos for even better effect. A Cash Back Orb can also be considered for more cost efficiency.

